<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Portsmouth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1905</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Portsmouth ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>. It was signed on 5 September 1905 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-new-hampshire">Portsmouth, New Hampshire</a>, after talks mediated by US President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s paramount interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, transferred its lease of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lushunkou">Port Arthur</a> and the railway south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changchun">Changchun</a>, and ceded southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>, but Japan received no indemnity, which provoked riots in Tokyo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-portsmouth.jpg" alt="Treaty of Portsmouth" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_Russia_Treaty_of_Peace_5_September_1905.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
